{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 47, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a ruler of the County of Tripoli a Latin state in the Levant in the 12th century\n\nHe was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and the heir to the county after his father died in 1152\n\nRaymond III took the throne in 1152 and was crowned by the Holy Roman Emperor Frederick I at the council of Piacenza in 1152\n\nThe council also decided that Raymond III should be recognized as the ruler of the County of Tripoli and the County of Sidon\n\n* **Conquests and Military Campaigns**\nRaymond III expanded the county's territories through military campaigns against the Fatimid Caliphate and the Ayyubid dynasty of Egypt\n\nHe conquered the city of Sidon in 1152 and the city of Beirut in 1154\n\nHe also allied himself with the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Holy Roman Empire to fight against the Ayyubid dynasty\n\nRaymond III played a key role in the Second Crusade and was a significant ally of the Crusaders\n\n* **Administrative and Economic Reforms**\nRaymond III implemented various administrative and economic reforms in the County of Tripoli\n\nHe established a new system of government with a council of advisors and a chancellor to oversee the administration of the county\n\nHe also established trade relationships with other European and Middle Eastern states and encouraged the growth of commerce in the county\n\nRaymond III encouraged the construction of new churches and monasteries and supported the establishment of the Knights Hospitaller in the county\n\n* **Personal Life and Death**\nRaymond III married Eschiva of Ibelin a member of the noble family of Ibelin and had several children with her\n\nHe died in 1187 at the Battle of Hattin where he was killed along with many other Crusaders\n\nThe Battle of Hattin marked the end of the County of Tripoli and the Kingdom of Jerusalem which had been weakened by internal conflic